# Molecular braids in metal–organic frameworks

## abstract
Molecular braids represent one of the most fascinating topological networks in the fields of supramolecular chemistry and crystal engineering. In this Tutorial Review we provide an overview of the sustainable exploration of those metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with the topology of molecular braid. Since the first example of the triple-stranded molecular braid in MOFs was reported by our group in 2005, another five attractive molecular braids in MOFs have already been prepared through several different pathways by us and other groups. Indeed, these known examples include the triple-, quintuple- and sextuple-stranded molecular braids. Besides the molecular braids found in MOFs, this type of unique topological network was also observed in organic co-crystals, and the only example of this is discussed in context as well.

## Molecular braids in metal–organic frameworksw
Molecular braids represent one of the most fascinating topological networks in the fields of supramolecular chemistry and crystal engineering. In this Tutorial Review we provide an overview of the sustainable exploration of those metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with the topology of molecular braid. Since the first example of the triple-stranded molecular braid in MOFs was reported by our group in 2005, another five attractive molecular braids in MOFs have already been prepared through several different pathways by us and other groups. Indeed, these known examples include the triple-, quintuple- and sextuple-stranded molecular braids. Besides the molecular braids found in MOFs, this type of unique topological network was also observed in organic co-crystals, and the only example of this is discussed in context as well.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), also named as coordination polymers or metal–organic coordination networks, are inorganic–organic solid-state hybrid crystalline materials with infinite network arrays.1 So far, a great number of MOFs have been proven to be potential functional materials for molecular selection, ion exchange, optical devices, catalysis and so on.2–4 Thereby, tremendous efforts have been put into the elaboration of useful MOFs by choosing suitable metal ions or cluster nodes (secondary building units, SBUs) together with functionally valuable organic linkers. In the process of various MOFs assembly, topological control has served as a facile and effective approach, because topology analysis could simplify complex structures to standard known frameworks and could also help to analyze the packing trends of diverse MOFs, which may guide and support the direct-synthesis of functional MOFs with desired features.5
The ever-increasing number of MOFs reported in the literature provides us with an excellent database for classifying the topologically versatile supramolecular structures.6 Many of these MOFs are particularly intriguing and pleasing due to the appearance of independent motifs being entangled together by interlocking or interweaving in different manners, and they are usually called entangled MOFs (EMOFs).
