Generally speaking, EMOFs could be divided into two categories: (i) the first one contains all separable components. That is, if one or more subunits are disconnected the other fragments will retain the original characteristic regime. Representative examples are multi-stranded helical chains (Scheme 1);9 (ii) the second one is formed by inseparable fragments. All components, which contain a closed circuit-like unit at least, interlock or interweave each other firmly. Representative examples include Catenane,10 Rotaxane,11 and Borromean rings (Scheme 2).12 A single Borromean ring is a discrete zero-dimensional (0D) structure generated by interlocking three independent rings. If one ring is unlocked, one 1D chain and two free separated rings will be obtained. When the three rings are all disconnected at the same position in a Borromean ring, three 1D chains will be formed. If these three chains expand along one direction and interweave with each other to give rise to constant Borromean nodes, a completely new topological system, which is called a molecular braid, will be generated (Scheme 3).
To our knowledge, the term of molecular braid was initially introduced by Hosseini and co-authors in an organic co-crystal complex,13 which actually exists as a quintuple-stranded helix through hydrogen bonding interactions, so it is inappropriate to call it a molecular braid. Logically, a molecular braid, which should mimic a hair braid, is formed by interweaving three or more independent 1D chains, and each two 1D chains do not interlace with each other, but the entire components do interlock together very well. Obviously, there are several crystallographic and topological differences between 1D molecular braids and a multiple-stranded helix: firstly, a single strand of the molecular braid is a 1D meso-helical chain with the inversion centers, whereas a single strand of the multiple-stranded helix is always a chiral helical chain which has definitive chirality; secondly, the helix is a chiral molecular structure with a screw axis, while the centrosymmetric molecular braid is achiral; thirdly, removal of any strand of the molecular braid will also release the other strands from it, while in a helix, the
