## 2. Molecular braids
## 2.1 The discrete molecular braids in MOFs
The first example of a triple-stranded molecular braid was reported by our group in 2005.14 The assembly of a highly flexible bridging ligand 1,3-bis(4-pyridyl)propane (bpp, all the ligands used in this context are listed in Table 1) and $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{maa})_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ ( $\mathrm{\cdotHmaa}=2$ -methylacrylic acid) in a molar ratio of 2 : 1 led to a molecular braid EMOF $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{4}(b p p)_{4}(m a a)_{8}.}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}{\cdot}2n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1). In this complex, $\mathrm{{Cu(n)}}$ centers exhibit three types of coordination environment (Fig. S1 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ): distorted square pyramid, ideal square planar, and distorted octahedral geometries, respectively; the flexible bpp ligands possess TT and TG conformations $(\mathrm{T}=t r a n s$ and $\mathrm{G}=g a u c h e)$ with respect to the relative orientation of the methylene groups. The incorporation of $\mathrm{{Cu(n)}}$ ions and bpp ligands gave rise to a 1D meso-helical chain (Fig. 1), in which the turning nodes are the methylene groups of the bpp ligands, and the 1D meso-helical chain can be annotated as the alternate units $\mathrm{\Delta[-bpp^{TT}-C u^{5}-b p p^{T G}-C u^{6}-}$ $\mathsf{b p p}^{\mathrm{TG}}\mathrm{-cu}^{5}\mathrm{-bpp}^{\mathrm{TT}}\mathrm{-cu}^{4}\mathrm{-}]_{n}$ with a periodicity of 44.280(2) A 1 $\mathrm{{Cu}}^{x}$ , $\scriptstyle x\ =$ coordination number). Clearly, these discrete 1D chains cannot be extended into a higher dimensional framework because of the auxiliary terminal maa ligands, while three independent chains are able to entangle together to generate a neutral interlaced triple-stranded molecular braid by the self-recognition hydrogen bonding interactions between one coordinated water and two oxygen atoms from different coordinated maa anions (Fig. 2). It is notable that the molar ratio of these two starting materials is extremely critical for the formation of this novel molecular braid EMOF, and the variation of this factor could not produce a similar molecular braid framework. For instance, when the molar ratio of bpp to $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(m a a)_{4}.2H_{2}O]}$ was changed to 1.5 : 1, a 1D zigzag-like chain MOF $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(m a a)_{6}(b p p)_{2}]_{\it n}}$ (2) was isolated under identical reaction conditions (Fig. S2 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ); the individual 1D chains of 2 with the mono- and dinuclear nodes were arranged in parallel with each other in the 2D layers, and each layer intercrossed the neighboring layer beneath at an angle of $53^{\circ}$ to form an interlaced 3D supramolecular structure (Fig. S3 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ).15 A further decrease of the molar ratio to 1 : 1 gave rise to a MOF $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(m a a)_{4}(b p p)]}_{n}$ (3), which also consists of 1D zigzag-like chains but based on the binuclear paddle-wheel nodes with the parallel stacking fashion (Fig. S4 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ .
The second example of the triple-stranded molecular braid was also prepared by our group. In contrast to the first one which is constructed based on a flexible bridging ligand, this unique case is furnished by using a completely rigid linear ligand biphenyl- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate $({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bpdc}})$ .15 The reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpdc}$ , $2{,}2^{\prime}{:}6^{\prime}{,}2^{\prime\prime}$ -terpyridine (tpy) and a $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ precursor gives the molecular braid EMOF $\mathrm{[Cd(bpdc)(tpy)]}_{n}{\cdot}2n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (4, Fig. S5 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). In the reaction system, the tpy ligand was introduced deliberately as the chelating terminal tecton to avoid the formation of higher dimensional architectures. In complex 4, bpdc ligands adopt two coordination fashions for Cd(II) ions: bis(chelating) and bis(monodentate) modes. The two benzene rings of bpdc ligands display a torsion angle of $44.41(2)^{\circ}$ in the former fashion, whereas they are almost coplanar in the latter, which results in the formation of a 1D meso-helical chain (Fig. 3). Similar to 1, the most striking structural feature of 4 is that it also contains a neutral, interlaced, triple-stranded braid architecture, which is assembled by interweaving three single 1D meso-helical chains (Fig. 4). However, due to the rigidity of bpdc ligands, $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions serve as the turning nodes for the formation of the molecular braid in 4, which differs from the feature of 1. The guest water molecules are also equally important for the assembly of 4. There are two kinds of guest water molecules in 4: the first are water dimers which help to link every two twisted single-stranded chains via hydrogen bonding interactions and also stabilize the molecular braid network; another type of water molecules are the single molecule bridges which are hydrogen-bonded to the oxygen atoms of bpdc ligands from the adjacent braids and thus permit the adjacent triple-stranded molecular braids to assemble into a 2D lamellar network.
The third triple-stranded molecular braid, namely $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{sd})_{6}(4,4^{\prime}-$ $\mathrm{bipy})_{3}]_{n}$ (5, Fig. S6 in ESIw), was synthesized by the reaction of salicylaldehyde (sd) and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine ( $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy) co-ligands with $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ salt.16 Using the same strategy as the molecular braid MOF of 4, the example is achieved by employing a rigid linear ligand as well. The extensive experiment attempts revealed that the successful preparation of complex 5 was mainly dependent on the reaction temperature control. In 5, the neutral fragments $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{sd})_{2}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy ligands are alternately linked to afford a neutral 1D meso-helical chain in which $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy ligands show two distinct stereo-configurations: one has the $D_{2\mathrm{h}}$ point group with the two pyridine planes being nearly co-planar, while the other presents the $C_{2\mathrm{v}}$ point group with the two pyridine planes featuring a distorted angle of $26.4^{\circ}$ (Fig. 5). Luckily, three identical 1D meso-helical chains interlace each other to form the triple-stranded molecular braids via the $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ interactions between the pyridine rings of interchain $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy ligands (Fig. 6). The structural feature of the molecular braid in 5 is quite different from those in 1 and 4. First of all, the $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ ions in 5 act as both the turning nodes and the bridging tectons for the assembly of the molecular braid; secondly, the two pseudo-loops of the six-membered rings (based on the calculation of the number of the metal ions) are interlocked each other in turn to yield the triple-stranded molecular braid (Fig. S7 in ESIw). These observations should be attributed to the length of the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy ligand in 5 is obviously shorter than that of either bpp or bpdc ligands. Thereby, the loops with more metal ions incorporated are required for the entanglement of those 1D meso-helical chains in 5.
Later on, two isostructural 1D sextuple-stranded molecular braid EMOFs $[\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{pcp})_{2}(\mathrm{bpp})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ $\mathbf{M}\ =\mathbf{\Gamma}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , 6; Co, 7; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pcp}\ =\ 1,3\mathrm{-b}$ is(4-carboxy-phenoxy)propane) were synthesized by the utilization of two kinds of bridging ligands $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pcp}$ and bpp with different metal precursors in a molar ratio of 1 : 1 : 1 as well.17 Herein, the molecular braid EMOF 6 is chosen to be discussed in detail. In complex 6, $\mathrm{{Cu(n)}}$ centers are linked by pcp ligands with bidentate-chelating and monodentate coordination modes and a bpp ligand with the TT and $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{G}^{\prime}$ conformations to build up a double-stranded chain with large windows (Fig. 7a and Fig. S8 in ESIw), which can be further interlocked by two other identical motifs to form a three-fold $\mathrm{1D~\rightarrow~1D}$ parallel interpenetration. This unusual interpenetration is facilitated by hydrogen bonding interactions between coordinated water molecules and coordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms of the adjacent 1D chains (Fig. 7b). In the three interweaving double-stranded chains, two different kinds of the triple-stranded molecular braids could be found: the first one is made up of bpp ligands and $\mathrm{{Cu(n)}}$ centers, while another type is established by the pcp ligands and $\mathrm{{Cu(n)}}$ nodes (Fig. 8). Finally, one triple-stranded molecular braid further entangles with another molecular braid of a different style to produce a novel sextuple-stranded molecular braid (Fig. 9).
It should be pointed out that those two braids in the sextuplestranded molecular braid are interconnected via sharing the same $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ centers to form the initial 1D double-stranded chains. In addition, when the amount of bpp ligand was reduced to half in the synthetic procedure, a new Co(II) EMOF $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{pcp})_{2}(\mathrm{bpp})]_{n}{\cdot}2n\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ (8) was isolated (Fig. S9 in $\mathrm{ESI\dagger}$ ).17 Different from the structural features of 6 and 7, complex 8 exhibits a 2D grid with the nominal sql net generated by pcp and bpp co-ligands linking the paddle-wheel $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{4}$ dimeric units (Fig. S10 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). Subsequently, three identical 2D sheets interpenetrated each other to produce an overall $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{D}}\ensuremath{\rightarrow}\ensuremath{3\mathrm{D}}$ triply parallel entangled motif involving 2-membered circuits (the interpenetration occurs through 2-membered circuits).
## 2.2 The molecular braid substructure in MOFs
Recently, molecular braids were also found to serve as the key secondary building blocks for the construction of higher dimensional MOFs. Wang and co-authors reported a selfpenetrated 3D EMOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\mathrm{bptc})_{2}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})_{4}]_{n}{\cdot}n(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{N})$  $4n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (9) by the combination of the long V-shaped $^{3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ -benzophenonetetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}b p t c)}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy co-ligands with $Z\mathrm{n(n)}$ ions (Fig. S11 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag},$ ).18 The 3D network of 9 consists of a substructural quintuplestranded molecular braid and two types of triple-stranded molecular braids. As depicted in Fig. 10a, the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy and benzophenone- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate groups of bptc co-ligands link $Z\mathrm{n(n)}$ centers to form a quintuple-stranded molecular braid extending along the $c$ axis. The first type of triple-stranded molecular braid is assembled by the benzophenone- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate groups of bptc ligands and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy ligands bridging $Z\mathrm{n(n)}$ centers (Fig. 10b), each triple-stranded molecular braid then further interweaves with the other two neighboring braids to give rise to a novel nine-fold interwoven meso-helical chain, whereas the second type of triple-stranded braid is generated by the benzophenone- $^{3,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate groups and benzophenone- $^{.4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate groups of bptc ligands with $^{4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ - bipy ligands bridging $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ units (Fig. 10c). Interestingly, herein one 1D chain of the second three-stranded braid is not fully situated below the other two 1D chains, which is different from the arrangement of the chains in the triplestranded braids in EMOFs 1, 4, 5, 6 and 7. A detailed structural study indicates that the two chains of the second three-stranded braid form a quadrilateral and narrow different ring-like spaces with window sizes of $8.5\times6.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $3.6\times$ $11\overline{{.6}}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , respectively, which allows the third chain to thread the quadrilateral window, thus generating a different chain arrangement fashion in the second three-stranded braid. The structure analysis of EMOF 9 suggests that the strong $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ stacking interactions play an important role in stabilizing the network of the molecular braids.
## 2.3 The molecular braids in organic co-crystals
Co-crystals are one kind of crystalline material containing different organic molecules which are, in their pure forms, solid under the ambient conditions, held together via noncovalent interactions. Recently, an interesting organic co-crystal (tbbps)(bpp) $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ (10) featuring the topology of a molecular braid was prepared by utilization of a brominated organic molecule tetrabromobisphenol-S (tbbps) and bpp co-ligands (Fig. S12 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ .19 In the tbbps ligand, the donor–donor distance $\mathrm{(O_{hydroxyl}.\cdot\cdot O_{h y d r o x y l})}$ is $\sim8.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ which is close to the acceptor–acceptor distance $(\mathsf{N}\cdot\cdot\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathsf{N}})$ of the bpp ligand $(8.5{-}9.5\mathring\mathrm{A})$ based on the TG or TT conformations. Importantly, the incorporation of electron-withdrawing Br substituents enhances the hydroxyl group in tbbps to be a more potent acceptor for the generation of hydrogen bonding interactions between these two organic building components. According to the knowledge of active pharmaceutical ingredients (API), organic co-crystals and the method of the module matching strategy, these two bent tbbps and bpp organic molecules are geometrically matched organic modules in the self-assembly process via $\mathrm{O_{hydroxyl}{\mathrm{-}}H{\cdot}\cdot N}$ hydrogen bonds adopting the head-to-tail fashion, giving rise to the 1D supramolecular meso-helical string with the alternate iteration of left- and right-handed half turns (Fig. 11). Consequently, the combination of three 1D chains generates an entangled triple-stranded molecular braid (Fig. 12). It should be noted that this unique organic co-crystal molecular braid is fully governed by the supramolecular interactions and different from all the aforementioned molecular braid EMOFs.
## 3. Conclusions
Complex molecules inspired from images in nature are extraordinarily challenging synthetic objectives. Over the past seven years, the notion of molecular braid chemical structures has advanced from being a largely hypothetical one to a realistic achievement with seven successful examples, including triple-, quintuple- and sextuple-stranded molecular braid networks.
So far, the success in the preparation and characterization of all kinds of MOFs has been fruitful. Accordingly, the molecular building blocks are becoming increasingly complex, and there are virtually an infinite number of possible combinations of organic linkers and metal ions (or metal clusters). Thereby, the isolation of more versatile molecular braid structures could be expected to be sustainable in the future. Based on the first hand experience, several potential influencing factors could be summarized and suggested: (i) flexible ligands might be slightly easier to direct the formation of molecular braids in comparison with rigid ligands; (ii) the appropriate turning angle of bridging ligands is mandatory to enhance the formation of 1D meso-helical chains and molecular braids; (iii) the appropriate non-covalent supramolecular interactions could facilitate the union of those individual chains. These discussed items should be kept in mind together when attempting to yield the various molecular braid EMOFs.
Despite the remarkable advancement of molecular braid MOFs in the past few years, these supramolecular structures have found nearly no applicable functions until now. By analyzing the known cases in the literature, it is seen that the distances between metal ions could be shortened greatly, being affected by the entanglement. Hopefully, this phenomenon might help molecular braids to become more promising functional materials, such as in magnetism, conductivity, et al. Of course, this type of unique molecular braid MOFs only just began to appear. Many more remain to be explored.
